Unity USD Toolkit patch to OpenUSD v26.05 (commit 2095fafafd033fa23386d7ec6d58c7cc33974518)
SECURITY-282834: "Bundled OpenUSD dependency parsers as vulnerable/unscanned components
(LZ4 1.9.2)", CWE-1104.

Applied by Native~/build_openusd.py to a worktree of the pinned commit, never to the clone
itself; its SHA-256 is pinned in Native~/dependency-sources/<platform>.tsv. Nothing else in the
OpenUSD tree is changed.

1. pxr/base/tf/pxrLZ4/lz4.{h,cpp}: LZ4 1.9.2 -> 1.10.0 (lib/lz4.h and lib/lz4.c at upstream
   tag v1.10.0, commit ebb370ca83af193212df4dcbadcc5d87bc0de2f0), with Pixar's nine
   "PXR - modification" edits re-applied exactly as Pixar made them to 1.9.2: C linkage
   removed, the <stdint.h> include hoisted, and the library wrapped in
   PXR_NAMESPACE / namespace pxr_lz4. No other line differs from upstream 1.10.0.
   CVE-2021-3520 -- a negative output capacity reaching memmove() in
   LZ4_decompress_generic -- is fixed upstream by commit 8301a21, first released in 1.9.4
   (not 1.9.3).

2. pxr/base/tf/fastCompression.cpp: TfFastCompression::DecompressFromBuffer checks every size
   it passes to LZ4, since all of them come from the file being read (the .usdc token, field
   and integer sections). Before this, the crate reader's 64-bit uncompressedSize reached LZ4
   as a negative int once it passed INT_MAX -- the CVE-2021-3520 trigger -- a chunk size was
   not checked against the buffer, and the chunk count was read as a signed char, so a first
   byte of 0x80 or more gave a negative count the loop never reached. Buffers written by
   CompressToBuffer satisfy every check, so valid files decompress exactly as before.

Upstream (PixarAnimationStudios/OpenUSD dev) still vendors LZ4 1.9.2 as of 2026-09-29.

